import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="How Auto Tone works — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="Auto Tone sets seven sliders from a photo's own histogram. Four of them are solved against the real editing pipeline, three are rules of thumb, and none of it is a trained model."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-26">September 26, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>How Auto Tone works</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          Auto Tone sets seven sliders from a photo's own histogram. Four are
          solved against the real editing pipeline, three are rules of thumb,
          and none of it is a trained model.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 7 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          Press Auto (or Cmd+U) and LightPhotos looks at a 256-pixel version
          of the photo and sets Exposure, Blacks, Whites, Contrast, Shadows,
          Highlights and Vibrance. It doesn't work out the right numbers with
          math. It guesses a slider value, runs it through the same pipeline
          the editor uses, checks the result, and halves the guess until the
          middle of the histogram, the darkest 1% and the brightest 1% land
          where they should.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first version got colourful photos badly wrong, and fixing that
          taught me more than writing it did. It takes about 7&nbsp;ms per
          photo, so a batch of thousands runs in the background while you keep
          working.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Let the computer find the number.</strong> Working out the
          exact slider value by hand means inverting every curve in the
          pipeline, and every change to the pipeline breaks it. Guessing and
          checking against the real pipeline stays correct for free.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>An average can lie about the thing it summarises.</strong>
          Treating a bucket of red pixels as "a gray of the same brightness"
          made saturated photos read twice as bright as they were.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A sorted list stops being sorted once you change the
          values.</strong> Brightening one colour more than another can swap
          their order, so the darkest 1% has to be found again for every
          guess.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Size a batch by what's in flight, not by what was
          asked.</strong> Asking for every thumbnail up front was fine for 50
          photos and asked the browser for about 8&nbsp;GB at 20,000.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What it's for</h2>
      <p>
        Most of my RAW files need the same few things: a bit more exposure,
        deeper blacks, a little less glare in the sky. Auto Tone is there to
        do that first pass so I start from something close and only nudge. It
        only touches the tone sliders. Your crop, white balance, saturation
        and noise reduction are left exactly as they were.
      </p>
      <p>
        The idea isn't mine. The overall shape comes from RapidRAW's auto
        adjust, an open-source editor. Its constants didn't carry over,
        because its sliders are built differently: its Blacks lifts only the
        pixels near black, while ours remaps the whole range. So I kept the
        shape and found new numbers.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 196" role="img" aria-label="The Auto Tone pipeline. The photo is shrunk to 256 pixels and turned into a 256-bin histogram. Exposure, Blacks, Whites and Contrast are solved three times over against the real pipeline. Then Shadows, Highlights and Vibrance are set by simple rules, and the result is saved as an ordinary edit.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">ONE PHOTO THROUGH AUTO TONE</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="28" width="140" height="44" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="70" y="47" text-anchor="middle">256 px sample</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="70" y="63" text-anchor="middle">linear light</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="140" y1="50" x2="158" y2="50"></line>
