          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="336" y="104" width="304" height="44" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="488" y="123" text-anchor="middle">Rules of thumb</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="488" y="139" text-anchor="middle">Shadows, Highlights, Vibrance</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="336" y1="126" x2="318" y2="126"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M318 121 L308 126 L318 131 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="168" y="104" width="140" height="44" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="238" y="123" text-anchor="middle">Saved edit</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="238" y="139" text-anchor="middle">original untouched</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="184">Other sliders (crop, white balance, saturation, denoise) are kept as you set them.</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <h2>Looking at a small copy</h2>
      <p>
        Auto Tone never reads the full-size file. For the photo you're looking
        at, it reuses the 256-pixel sample the histogram already has. For
        everything else, it shrinks the cached 512-pixel thumbnail down to the
        same 256 pixels, so a photo gets the same answer whether you toned it
        on its own or in a batch of a thousand.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each pixel goes through the editor's own pipeline with every slider at
        zero, so Auto Tone sees the photo the way you see it on screen.
        Brightness goes into one of 256 bins. Each bin also keeps the average
        colour of the pixels in it, which turned out to matter a lot.
      </p>

      <h2>Guessing instead of deriving</h2>
      <p>
        Four sliders each have one job:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li><strong>Exposure</strong> puts the middle of the histogram at 50% brightness.</li>
        <li><strong>Blacks</strong> puts the darkest 1% at 1%.</li>
        <li><strong>Whites</strong> puts the brightest 1% at 99%.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contrast</strong> only kicks in if Blacks and Whites still
          couldn't spread the photo far enough. A well-exposed photo keeps
          Contrast at zero.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The targets are 1% and 99% rather than 0% and 100% because aiming at
        the very ends would clip 1% of the photo to pure black and pure white.
      </p>
      <p>
        To hit each target, Auto Tone uses the oldest trick there is:
        bisection. Try the middle of the slider's range, run the histogram
        through the pipeline, see whether the result is too dark or too
        bright, and throw away the wrong half. Twenty-four halvings of a
        &minus;100 to +100 slider gets within a hundred-thousandth.
      </p>
      <pre class="lp-code">lo, hi = slider range
repeat 24 times:
    mid = (lo + hi) / 2
    if measure(photo with slider = mid) &lt; target: lo = mid
    else: hi = mid</pre>
      <p>
        The sliders affect each other, so it does Exposure, Blacks, Whites and
        Contrast in order and then goes round twice more. Three passes was
        enough for every photo I tried.
      </p>
      <p>
        This only works if a slider always moves its measure in one direction:
        more Exposure never makes the middle darker. There are tests that check
        exactly that, on both the ordinary and RAW pipelines, because if it
        stops being true the search quietly lands on the wrong number.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first version's notes said Blacks and Whites were worked out
        "in closed form" by a function that didn't exist. The code had always
        been bisecting them. It's a good example of why I read the code, not
        the summary.
      </p>

      <h2>The red-pixel bug</h2>
      <p>
        Bisection is cheap only because it doesn't re-run every pixel for
        every guess. Each of the 256 bins sends one stand-in through the
        pipeline, weighted by how many pixels it holds. In the first version
        that stand-in was a gray with the bin's average brightness.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's wrong for colour. Pure red appears about 30% bright on screen,
        but a gray carrying the same amount of light appears about 58%
        bright. On a very saturated test photo, Auto Tone measured the middle
        of the histogram at twice its real brightness and stopped with the
        photo still too dark. The solved middle sat at 0.15 when the target
        was 0.5.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 156" role="img" aria-label="Pure red displays at about 0.30 brightness. A gray carrying the same amount of light displays at about 0.58. Using the gray as a stand-in made saturated photos read too bright. After the fix, the solved middle of a saturated test photo moved from 0.15 to 0.53 against a target of 0.50.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">SAME LIGHT, DIFFERENT BRIGHTNESS ON SCREEN</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="44">Pure red</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad" x="130" y="32" width="150" height="16" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="290" y="44">0.30</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="72">Gray stand-in</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="130" y="60" width="290" height="16" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="430" y="72">0.58, reads too bright</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="0" y1="92" x2="640" y2="92"></line>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="114">SOLVED MIDDLE ON A SATURATED PHOTO (TARGET 0.50)</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="6" cy="136" r="4"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="18" y="140">Gray stand-ins: 0.15</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-good" cx="206" cy="136" r="4"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="218" y="140">Colour stand-ins: 0.53</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The fix was to keep a real colour for each bin: the average colour of
        its pixels, scaled down until it appears exactly as bright as the bin
        says. The scaling matters too. Averaging in linear light and then
        converting for the screen reads up to 8% brighter than the pixels
        really are, and that alone was enough to make Exposure stop short.
        After the fix, the saturated photo landed at 0.53.
      </p>

      <h2>Sorted lists don't stay sorted</h2>
      <p>
        The first version also sorted the bins once and assumed the order
        held: if bin A was darker than bin B before the edit, it would be
        darker after. For grays that's true. For colours it isn't. Push Whites
        to +100 and a bright red can end up darker than a gray it used to
        outshine.
      </p>
      <p>
        So now every guess re-sorts the 256 stand-ins by their new brightness
        before finding the middle and the 1% ends. That's 256 pipeline runs
        and a sort per guess, still tiny next to running every pixel. The test
        for this builds a scene where red and gray swap places and checks the
        answer against every real pixel.
      </p>

      <h2>The three sliders I eyeballed</h2>
      <p>
        Shadows, Highlights and Vibrance don't have a clean target, so they're
        rules of thumb. The code says so plainly: "Hand-picked rules with
        eyeballed constants. Retune by eye."
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Shadows</strong> lifts when more than 5% of the photo is
          near black.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Highlights</strong> pulls down when more than 2% is near
          white.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vibrance</strong> adds a little colour to very muted
          photos.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There's one guard that matters more than the rules. If the photo
        already has blown highlights, Exposure can only go down. An auto
        button that brightens a clipped sky is worse than no button.
      </p>

      <h2>Doing thousands at once</h2>
      <p>
        Cmd+Shift+U tones the whole selection. Each photo takes about 7&nbsp;ms
        to analyse, and it runs on the main thread, so a batch works through
        at most 5&nbsp;ms of analysis per frame and the app never stutters.
        Thumbnails load in the background, at most 32 at a time. That number
        comes from measurement: a slow RAW thumbnail can take 137&nbsp;ms, so
        you need about 17 in flight to keep the analysis fed, and 32 leaves
        margin for about 13&nbsp;MB.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before that window existed, a batch asked for every thumbnail at once.
        On the desktop that meant thumbnails could be thrown out of the cache
        before Auto Tone got to them. In the browser, a 20,000-photo selection
        asked for roughly 8&nbsp;GB in a memory space that tops out at 4.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two smaller rules keep batches honest. If you edit a photo by hand
        while it's waiting in the batch, Auto Tone skips it. If you switch
        folders, the batch is cancelled, so a late result can't land on a
        same-named photo in the new folder.
      </p>

      <h2>What it doesn't do</h2>
      <p>
        It doesn't touch white balance, and it looks at the whole frame even
        if you've cropped. There's no undo for it yet either. Reset or the
        sliders get you back. Those are the next things I'd like to fix.
      </p>
      <p>
        If Auto Tone does something odd to one of your photos, I'd love to
        see it. Open an issue on the <a
        href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">issue
        tracker</a> with the photo if you can share it.
      </p>

